course: dictionary
lesson: 4826
title: One word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word hurry can be a doing word (a verb) or a naming word (a noun). In «Hurry up, or we will miss the bus!» hurry is a verb: it means move quickly because there is not much time. In «We are in a hurry this morning» hurry is a noun: it means a need to be fast.
explain: The words around hurry give you clues. «There is no hurry, so take your time» tells you that hurry means being quick, and no hurry means you can go slowly. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
explain: Adverts often use hurry to make you act fast without thinking. «Hurry, only today!» is trying to rush you into buying something. Stop and ask yourself: do I really need this, or does the advert want me to hurry?
fact: Hurry can be a verb, as in «hurry home», or a noun, as in «in a hurry».
fact: To hurry means to move or do something quickly because there is not much time.
fact: To hurry someone means to make them go faster, as in «hurry up».
fact: In safety lessons, hurrying causes accidents, so never hurry across a road, run near a pool or rush down stairs.
fact: «Hurry, only today!» is designed to rush you, so take a breath before you buy.
exercise: In an advert, «Hurry, only today!» is trying to do what? | Make the price go up forever | Make the shop close early | Tell you the shop is slow | *Make you act quickly before you think
exercise: Safety rule: what should you never do when crossing a road? | *Hurry across it | Use the crossing | Look both ways | Wait for the green man
exercise: Which phrase means you have plenty of time? | *No hurry | Hurry home | In a hurry | Hurry up
test: In «we are in a hurry», what kind of word is hurry? | Adverb | *Noun | Adjective | Verb
test: What does it mean to hurry someone? | To stop them moving | To hug them | *To make them go faster | To tell them a story
exam: Which sentence uses hurry as a noun? | Hurry up now | Please hurry | *They left in a hurry | She will hurry to the shop
